# Keep kdump-tools off cell1 permanently. Install to
# /etc/apt/preferences.d/no-kdump-tools
#
# WHY A PIN AND NOT JUST A PURGE. kdump-tools is a *Recommends* of
# ubuntu-desktop-minimal, not a Depends. Purging it works and breaks
# nothing — but Recommends are installed by default, so the next
# `apt install` or release upgrade that touches the desktop metapackage
# would quietly pull it back, and with it
# /etc/default/grub.d/kdump-tools.cfg and the 512 MB reservation.
#
# That return would be invisible: no error, no prompt, just 512 MB
# fewer next boot and nothing pointing at why. A negative pin is the
# only thing that makes the removal stick.
#
# WHAT WE ARE GIVING UP. kdump captures a memory image when the kernel
# panics, for post-mortem analysis. On cell1 it was never armed —
# USE_KDUMP=0 (Ubuntu's default), `kdump-config show` read "Not ready
# to kdump", and /sys/kernel/kexec_crash_loaded was 0 while
# kexec_crash_size held the full 512 MB. So the reservation was paid
# for a capability that could not fire.
#
# The box has also never crashed: ten recorded boots, every one ending
# in "Journal stopped", the normal clean-shutdown message.
#
# TO REVERSE: delete this file, `apt install kdump-tools`, and set
# USE_KDUMP=1 in /etc/default/kdump-tools — that last step being the
# one that was missing all along.

Package: kdump-tools
Pin: release *
Pin-Priority: -1
